1. List your processes
bash#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Displays a list of currently running processes
ps -aux -H
a = all users' processes, u = user-oriented format, x = include processes without a TTY, -H (or f) = show process hierarchy.

2. Show your Bash PID
bash#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Displays lines containing the bash word from running processes
# shellcheck disable=SC2009
ps -aux | grep bash
The third line disables SC2009 (ShellCheck would suggest pgrep, but the task forbids it). grep bash filters the ps output to lines mentioning bash, so you can read off the Bash PID.

3. Show your Bash PID made easy
bash#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Displays the PID and process name of processes containing the word bash
pgrep -l bash
ps is forbidden here, so pgrep is the tool. -l lists both the PID and the process name. This matches the expected output format (4404 bash).

4. To infinity and beyond
bash#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Displays "To infinity and beyond" indefinitely
while true
